[{:seon.task/name "a-search-allocates-in-proportion"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-23 in the vocab session's full run (bin/test --all, 4 shards, loaded): seon.vector-cost-test/a-search-reads-each-row-once went red on bytes, which grew as n^1.96 where linear allows n^1.88 (the fit's increments basis over sizes 500, 1,000 and 2,000), at estimate ratios of 1.04, 1.02 and 1.00x. Its log was overwritten. It was green alone, and in five reruns of seon.vector-cost-test plus seon.program-cost-test at SEON_COST_MAX_N=100000 beside process-heavy bin/test runs (load 6.5 to 14.8), on main's judge (worktree session store). The increments basis applies only when the growth is at least seon.cost/growth-share of the smallest size's bytes. With the estimate's measured numbers (5.92 MB at 500, 6.18 MB at 1,000, 6.85 MB at 2,000) it is about 16%, so the bounded-fixed basis judges it, green. In the red run the bytes therefore really differed: the growth was larger or the base smaller. Not the coarse row buffer: seon.vector/near allocates it in an ffi arena, native memory the thread's byte count does not see. What the count sees grows per element: rows (into [] over (d/datoms db :aevt :seon.vector/coarse)), the keep? set (into #{} over :aevt of :of), and the datoms walk itself, whose node reads may depend on the index cache's state in that process. Next, when it is red again (its log kept): measure near's bytes at the three sizes in the same process before and after the shard's other tests, and split them between rows, keep? and the walk. Parked."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.vector-cost-test/a-search-reads-each-row-once}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-text-written-on-a-branch-is-embedded-there"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists (docs/vector.md#what-is-embedded): forms, sections and task plans are embedded on main by `seon.vector/vectors-proc` at the writer's commit notice; an agent's branch reads main's vectors as of its fork, so a plan the agent rewrites with `my.task/update!`, a function it defines, or a section text it changes has no vector until it merges. So `my.agent/similar` on its own new function answers [], and anything ranked by the plan's vector (the story's :similar, the related sections of `seon.context/the-screen-shows-the-sections-nearest-the-plan`) cannot follow the plan as the agent refines it over its run. An agent already reaches the model: `my.agent/similar` asks root's :embed effect over its socket (`agent/fx! :embed`), and the answer is plain data. The change: at the end of each turn's evals, the agent computes ```seon.vector/due``` on its branch (texts whose vector is missing or made from an earlier text: the plan changed, a new form) restricted to what the branch wrote since its fork, asks :embed once for all of them (batched as the effect already batches), and transacts ```seon.vector/tx-data``` on its branch; a failed embedding is the effect's recorded error and the texts stay due. Main is unchanged: a branch's vectors merge with its entities (check that the merge carries `:seon.vector/*` facts, or that main re-embeds the merged texts, whichever the merge already does; say which). Decided (2026-10-25, this worktree): the merge carries no :seon.vector/* facts (platform records: `seon.merge/carried-tx` carries program keys and the plan and instructions only), so main's `seon.vector/vectors-proc` embeds the merged texts again at the merge commit's notice, as any text new on main; carrying them would add the vector attributes to the carried set for one ~10 ms embedding per text saved. The candidates are ```seon.vector/written``` (a datoms-diff from the db the last call read, at first the fork's base: the cost of what changed, not of the store) plus the task's own plan (main may not have embedded a plan made just before the fork), judged by ```seon.vector/due``` over those datoms only (one seek each); ```seon.agent/embed-written!``` runs at the start of each turn's observe, before the screen is made, and takes its count from :seon.config.vector/texts (0, bin/test's roots: asks nothing). No new attribute, no thread in the agent (the model stays in root). Cost proof: a turn that writes nothing due asks nothing."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.vector-test/a-plan-rewritten-on-a-branch-gets-its-vector-there seon.vector-test/a-turn-with-nothing-due-asks-nothing}}
 {:seon.task/name "one-scan-ranks-every-kind"
  :seon.task/plan "Each ```seon.vector/near``` call reads every coarse row of the store (`d/datoms :aevt :seon.vector/coarse`) and only then drops those whose entity lacks its :of attribute, so a caller ranking several kinds against one query scans every vector once per kind. ```seon.context/related``` ranks three (forms by :form/id, wiki sections by :seon.section/address, schema keys by :schema/key) on each screen whose plan's vector changed: three scans of all n coarse rows where one is the work. Measured (2026-10-25, seon.context-cost-test/related-ranks-each-kind-by-one-search): 8.18 MB at 500 sections and 8.92 MB at 1,000, ~1.48 KB a row, ~2.1 times near's own per-row 700 B (seon.vector-cost-test/a-search-reads-each-row-once): the rows read again by each kind's scan. The change: near takes its :of as a set of attributes and answers each kind's ranking from the one scan (each coarse row read and copied into the kernel's buffer once, the C coarse scan run per kind over its own rows, or once with the rows partitioned by kind), and related asks it once. What a plain REPL would do: one pass. No new function: near's arity grows by the map's :of accepting a set; callers passing one attribute are unchanged. Cost proof: related's per-n bytes equal near's one-kind per-n bytes, not three times them.
Measured 2026-10-25 (worktree one-scan-ranks-every-kind, seon.profile/alloc-bytes over seon.context-cost-test/related-of-changed at 500 and 1,000 sections, per row from the difference): the premise is wrong. Three near calls cost 1.48 KB a row and one near of sections alone 1.44 KB: the second and third coarse scans add ~40 B a row. A row's cost is reading the coarse datoms (near with no :of, 589 B) and the :of kind's membership set, its attribute's datoms read into a set (791 B for :seon.section/address); each is paid once in either shape. The one-scan shape built (near's :of as a set, {attribute ranking}, related asking once; scratchpad one-scan.diff) measured 1.71 KB a row and 0.45 MB less fixed: the rows kept as a vector and then filtered per kind cost more than the scans saved. Not merged. What would lower the per-row bytes is the membership read: a vector that names its kind (a fact beside :seon.vector/coarse, or the coarse rows of each kind under their own attribute) would let one AEVT read of that kind's rows replace both the coarse scan and the set; that is a schema change, for root's gate. Otherwise this task is done as measured: related's cost proof stands at 1.48 KB a row, with this as its reason."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.context-cost-test/related-ranks-each-kind-by-one-search seon.vector-cost-test/a-search-reads-each-row-once}}]
